sections.s2_technical:
A big-bang rewrite — build the whole new service, then switch everything over on one date — is one of the highest-risk moves in software, because all the risk is concentrated into a single moment with no gradual learning and often no safe way back. The strangler-fig pattern spreads that risk across many small, reversible steps. You introduce a routing facade in front of the legacy service so that, from the callers' perspective, nothing changes — they still call the same address. Behind the facade, you decide per request where it goes: initially everything to the legacy, then progressively more to the new service.

The first job is to find a boundary where traffic or responsibility can be split cleanly. Good boundaries are a single endpoint, a bounded capability (balance enquiry, statement generation), a specific consumer, or a traffic percentage. You pick the smallest, lowest-risk slice first — ideally a read-only operation — so an error there is cheap and recoverable.

```java
// Strangler facade: route per capability. Start with the lowest-risk read.
Response route(Request r) {
    if (r.capability().equals("balance-enquiry") && flags.newEnabled("balance-enquiry"))
        return newService.handle(r);     // migrated slice
    return legacyService.handle(r);       // everything else still legacy
}
```

Before trusting the new service, you de-risk with shadow traffic and parallel validation. Shadow traffic means the facade sends the real request to both the legacy and the new service, returns the legacy response to the caller, and discards the new response — but logs and compares them. This exercises the new service under real production load and data without any customer impact. Parallel validation compares the two responses and flags any divergence, so you discover behavioural differences before the new service is ever authoritative.

```java
// Shadow + parallel validation: new service runs on real traffic, response NOT used yet
Response legacyResp = legacyService.handle(r);
Response shadowResp = tryShadow(() -> newService.handle(r)); // isolated, never thrown to caller
if (!equivalent(legacyResp, shadowResp))
    log.warning("DIVERGENCE on " + r.id() + " — investigate before cutover");
return legacyResp; // caller still gets the trusted legacy answer
```

When divergence is zero and confidence is high for a slice, you cut that slice over: the facade starts returning the new service's response for that capability, often ramped by percentage (one percent, ten percent, fifty percent, one hundred percent) so you can watch metrics at each step. A compatibility layer handles any contract differences, translating the new service's request and response shapes to what callers expect, so callers do not have to change in lockstep. You then repeat the cycle for the next slice until the legacy service serves nothing.

The part the question emphasises most is the return path. Every increment must include a designed, fast, rehearsed way back to the legacy service. In practice that is a feature flag or routing rule you can flip instantly to send the slice back to legacy, plus the guarantee that the legacy service is still fully functional and its data is still current — which means that during the migration, writes often must go to both systems or the systems must share a source of truth, so that flipping back does not lose data. The reason this must be designed in, not improvised, is that the whole justification for incremental migration is reversibility: if a step cannot be undone quickly, it is a big-bang in disguise. A staff-plus engineer treats "how do we get back" as a first-class requirement of each increment, with the rollback tested before the cutover, because discovering mid-incident that there is no safe way back is the exact failure the strangler-fig pattern exists to prevent.

